      "title": "Les langues",
      "description": "Say which languages you speak — and how well.",
      "estimatedMinutes": 11,
      "sections": [
        {
          "type": "learn",
          "title": "Learn",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Talking about languages",
              "body": "\"Parler\" means \"to speak\". Names of languages are masculine (le français, l'anglais), but after \"parler\" you usually drop the article: Je parle français. Add \"un peu\" (a little), \"bien\" (well) or \"très bien\" (very well) to say how well you speak.",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "Je parle français.", "english": "I speak French." },
                { "french": "Je parle un peu espagnol.", "english": "I speak a little Spanish." },
                { "french": "Elle parle très bien anglais.", "english": "She speaks English very well." },
                { "french": "Il parle bien italien.", "english": "He speaks Italian well." },
                { "french": "J'apprends le japonais.", "english": "I am learning Japanese.", "note": "after \"apprendre\", keep the article: le japonais" }
              ],
              "tip": "Like nationality adjectives, language names never take a capital letter in French: le français, l'anglais."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "vocabulary",
          "title": "Vocabulary",
